A||||8||Jhonny||Windshield molding replacement.||||1||
Z||000000||Jhonny||11-02-2000||05:26 AM||||<BR>Is the windshield trim molding glued or is it<BR>press fit in place ?<BR>The molding seems need replacement because the upper corners stand out. <BR>The windshield itself was replaced by a P.O<BR>but the molding wasn't.<P>/// Jhonny||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tigger||11-02-2000||08:41 AM||||If you pull on it slowly it should come out.  When I removed my old one it seem to be just pressed in, but it looked like the oozed out windshiled glue helped to hold it in and there was some places where it looked like the previous owner tried to use silicone to hold it down.<P>I'd check with a auto windsheild shop for new trim, they should be able to tell you if you need to glue it in.  Look for the wider 3/8 or 1/2 sized trim.  I've found it seals better and shouldn't pull up in the corners with age.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||GarethWright||11-02-2000||04:07 PM||||The Fiero Store has a windshield Molding for $20.85 its part# 50158.  I think thats what you mean anyway  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Mine is coming up on the top-passenger side corner, doesnt appear to be letting water in though, just looks kinda nasty.<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
Z||000003||ROB MAC||11-02-2000||05:46 PM||||When mine was replaced it was set in the WET polyurethane sealer( windshield was replaced) and then masking tape held it down until the sealer set.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||11-02-2000||07:00 PM||||You'll want to heat the molding to make it nearly rubber when you install it, otherwise you can't make the nice turns on the corners and it will pull away. Once it is soft, immediately install it and tape it down. <P>I drop it the hot engine for 5 minutes. It becomes very soft. I don't recommend anyone else do that since it could burn the car down and that's a bad thing....<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||11-02-2000||11:22 PM||||Just information..... the trim is for apperance and to protect the edge of the glass. It's not ment to seal anything. That's the job of the glue. If there's a leak then the adhesive has failed someplace along either the glass or the roof.<P>You don't want to run a car w/o the trim if you can avoid it. Knicking the edge of the windshield is a really easy way to start a big crack. The edge can be really delicate that way.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jhonny||11-03-2000||02:11 AM||||<BR>It's just the molding that looks ugly so I will replace it right away.<P>Thanks <P>/// Jhonny ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Jhonny||11-06-2000||09:53 AM||||<BR>I just visited my local glass shop to ask if they could replace the windshield trim.<BR>He said that he propably needed to remove glass. Is this really necessary ? <BR>He also said that he'd recommend to install a<BR>seal that wraps around the edge of the glass.<BR>This is common on other cars he said.<BR>Any opinions are welcome.<P>/// Jhonny<P>------------------<BR>Jhonny Sokura<BR>Vadstena, Östergötland Sweden<BR>http://www.vadstena.se/turism/engelsk/index.html||||reg||5
Z||000008||mbramble||11-06-2000||10:41 AM||||You do not need to remove the windshield to replace the moulding strip.  <P>The moulding acts as a sort of 'edge seal' since it protects the edge of the glass, as noted by Ogre above.<P>Just remove the old seal and take that to the glass shop and ask if they can replace it. Then install it yourself.  Even just removing and re-installing the same piece may improve the way it looks.  They may just have done a poor install after the glass was replaced by the previous owner.  After putting my old strip back in (after installing a sunroof), it looked like crap at the corners.  So, I removed it again, soaked it the bathtub in hot water and the put it back in.  Looks fine now.||||reg||1
